<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Ballad · War and march · 1686 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Lillibullero</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A tune of the 1680s used for a mocking political song in England; later the BBC's world radio signal and an army march.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Thomas Wharton</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Henry Purcell (attributed; he may have arranged rather than composed it)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>English Whigs and Protestants at the time of the Glorious Revolution; later British broadcasting and the army</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>England</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>tune first published 1686; Wharton's words 1686; broadsides and wide popularity 1688</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Ballad · War and march</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (lyrics by Thomas Wharton, 1686; tune attributed to Henry Purcell, first published 1686)</dd></div></dl>
<p class="kd-note">This song is about hard things from the past, like war, cruelty or death. You can read about it with a grown-up.</p><button type="button" class="kd-story sg-kd-open">Read about this song</button>
<div class="sg-body sg-fold">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>The melody of &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lillibullero">Lillibullero</a>&quot; is attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-purcell">Henry Purcell</a>, though it is unclear whether he composed it or arranged it; it was first published in The Delightful Companion, John Playford's method book for the recorder, in 1686, and no original <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> survives. In the same year Thomas, Lord Wharton, wrote words to it satirising James II's appointment of the Catholic Richard Talbot, 1st Earl of Tyrconnell, as Lord Deputy of Ireland. The song is a mocking dialogue between two Irishmen about Talbot's arrival and what it would mean for the Protestants, who feared a massacre like that of the Irish Rebellion of 1641, when &quot;Lilliburlero&quot; had been a watchword of the Irish Catholics. Its nonsense refrain has been read as garbled Irish, perhaps alluding to a prophecy of the astrologer William Lilly; the song mocks both Irish faith in prophecy and English trust in Providence.</p><p>At first it made little impression. When James II began moving Irish regiments to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in 1688, the words were printed as broadsides, and by October the song was immensely popular, sung in the streets and especially in English barracks to mock the arriving Irish soldiers. The Whigs all but adopted it as their anthem in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glorious-revolution">Glorious Revolution</a>. When William of Orange invaded the next month it was everywhere, said to be sung even by James's own palace guards, and a second part, rougher still, followed as William advanced. Wharton boasted that he had &quot;sung a deluded Prince out of three kingdoms&quot;. Parodies answered it, among them an anti-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> song in circulation by 17 November.</p><p>The tune long outlived the quarrel. John Gay used it in The Beggar's Opera in 1728; Laurence Sterne printed its score in Tristram Shandy, where Uncle Toby whistles it; it carried the English drinking song &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nottingham">Nottingham</a> Ale&quot;, sung at the launch of the East Indiaman Nottingham in 1787, and a nursery rhyme. In Ulster it became the tune of &quot;Protestant Boys&quot;, played by flute bands at Orange Order parades, which were a subject of controversy during the Troubles. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the BBC used it for the programme Into Battle, and it became the unofficial march of the British Army's Commandos; the BBC World Service then used it for decades as its interval signal before the news, chosen by engineers who found it carried well through short-wave noise, until a new theme by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> Lowe was introduced in 2008; a shortened version is still sometimes played before the hourly news. It is the regimental march of the Royal Electrical and Mechanical Engineers and of their Canadian and Australian counterparts.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A satire in the voices of two Irish Catholics who welcome Talbot's appointment and expect their nation's power and lands to be restored. It mocks them, and by playing on Protestant fears turned a political crisis into a marching joke. Its mood is jeering and lively.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">The words</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Ho! broder Teague, dost hear de decree?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Lilliburlero bullen a la.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Dat we shall have a new deputie.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Lilliburlero bullen a la.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Lero lero, lilli burlero, lero lero, bullen a la,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Lero lero, lilli burlero, lero lero, bullen a la.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Ho! by shaint Tyburn, it is de Talbote,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And he will cut all de English troate.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Dough by my shoul de English do praat,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>De law's on dare side, and Creish knows what.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>But if dispence do come from de pope,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>We'll hang Magna Charta, and dem in a rope:</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>For de good Talbot is made a lord,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And with brave lads is coming aboard:</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Who all in France have taken a sware</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Dat dey will have no protestant heir.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Ara! but why does he stay behind?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Ho! by my shoul 'tis a protestant wind.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>But see, de Tyrconnel is now come ashore,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And we shall have commissions gillore.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And he dat will not go to mass</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Shall be turn out, and look like an ass.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>But now de hereticks all go down,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>By Creish and shaint Patrick, de nation's our own.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Dare was an old prophesy found in a bog,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>'Ireland shall be rul'd by an ass, and a dog.'</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And now dis prophesy is come to pass,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>For Talbot's de dog, and Ja..s is de ass.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Words: Thomas Wharton (1687), as printed in Grove's Dictionary of Music and Musicians (1900). Text from <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/A_Dictionary_of_Music_and_Musicians%2FLilliburlero" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p></div></section>
</div>
